{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 34, "model_name": "abacusai/dracarys-llama-3.1-70b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was the Count of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187 and Prince of Galilee and Tiberias from 1174 to 1187. He was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem.\n\n*Early Life*\nRaymond III was born around 1140 in Tripoli Lebanon. His father Raymond II was Count of Tripoli and his mother Hodierna was the daughter of Baldwin II King of Jerusalem and Morphia of Melitene. Raymond III had a younger brother named Melisende who married the lord of Chastelneuf.\n\n*Reign*\nRaymond III succeeded his father as Count of Tripoli in 1152. He was a minor at the time and the regency was held by his mother Hodierna. In 1157 Raymond III married Eschiva of Bures daughter of William of Bures and Eschiva of Saint Omer. The marriage was likely arranged by King Baldwin III of Jerusalem to strengthen the alliance between Tripoli and Jerusalem.\n\n*Conflict with the Kingdom of Jerusalem*\nRaymond III had a strained relationship with the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He was involved in a dispute with King Amalric I of Jerusalem over the succession to the throne of Jerusalem. Raymond III claimed that he was the rightful heir to the throne as the grandson of Baldwin II but Amalric I was crowned king instead. The dispute led to a conflict between the two kingdoms and Raymond III formed an alliance with Nur ad-Din the ruler of Aleppo.\n\n*Capture and Release*\nIn 1164 Raymond III was captured by the forces of Nur ad-Din during the Battle of Harim. He was held prisoner for several years and was released in 1174 after paying a ransom. After his release Raymond III returned to Tripoli and resumed his rule as count.\n\n*Later Life and Death*\nRaymond III died on September 1187 during the Battle of Hattin. He was succeeded by his nephew Raymond IV Count of Tripoli.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.004263356793671846}, {"token":